SPEC §5 'Packed layout', §7. New option layout => 'depth' (default, unchanged) | 'packed'. Packed: dependencies play no part in placement; cells sorted by namespace/name fill rows left to right, top to bottom; positions stay put while the set of Combs is the same. Grid by precedence: an explicitly given columns, else rows, else aspect (width/height; pick the column count whose honeycomb bounding box comes closest), aspect default 16/9. Careful: columns has a default of 6 for the depth layout - packed must tell 'given' from 'default' (predicate), otherwise rows and aspect never apply. Groups still work per group_label, each group its own packed block. edges default to 0 in packed, an explicit edges => 1 wins. CLI: --layout, --rows, --aspect (accept 16:9 and 1.78). Tests: placement ignores dependsOn; columns/rows/aspect each; precedence; stability when only phases change; groups; edges default; a wide and a tall aspect give different grids; depth layout output is byte-identical to before. Add a second demo: examples/monitor.svg from the same demo.json in packed layout, with its freshness test, and show it in the README.

2026-10-02 04:27 Decisions where SPEC 5/7 is open (worker):
1. aspect is measured on the whole canvas, not the bare honeycomb: Layout gets frame_width/frame_height (default 0, counted only when choosing columns, never in its result) and the facade passes padding + title band + ONE legend row. Reason: purpose is filling a wall screen; with the bare honeycomb the 15-Comb demo came out 1.36:1 for a 16/9 target, with the frame 1.68:1. Not accounted: a legend wrapping to more rows, a title wider than the honeycomb.
2. aspect picks ONE column count for all groups (stacked blocks + headings measured together), closest by log ratio, tie -> fewer columns. Per-group aspect would stack several 16:9 blocks into a tall picture.
3. rows holds per group block: columns = ceil(cells/rows), so a block has at most 'rows' rows and fewer when the cells do not fill them (9 cells, rows 4 -> 3 columns -> 3 rows). columns likewise per block.
4. Sort is by cell id (namespace/name, or name alone) as a string - Layout knows only id/name/group/dependencies.
5. Given vs default columns: _columns_given set in BUILD from the constructor args, in facade and Layout; public columns accessor still answers 6. In depth layout the facade passes columns always (a subclass default override keeps working).
6. Bad option values (layout => 'spiral', rows => 0, aspect => '16:9' as string in Perl) die in the constructor through the isa check - exactly how columns/size/edges behave today and what the POD of render documents; the CLI checks them first and exits 1 with one line. No fall back to default: it would be the only option doing so.
7. Layout names the attribute 'mode', not 'layout' - 'layout' is its main method. Facade option is 'layout' as in the SPEC.
8. edges became a lazy attribute (builder: 0 in packed, else 1); the CLI no longer passes edges unless --edges/--no-edges is given.
9. demo.pl writes monitor.svg next to demo.svg (also when a path is given, so t/50-demo.t writes nothing into the tree). monitor.svg: packed, no group_label, default aspect.

